{
  "title": "Pangea™ Distal Lateral Fibula Plate",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/implants/423205/pangea-tm--distal-lateral-fibula-plate",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 8 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "Related Implants",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea™ series contains 3 distal fibula plates",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**Distal Lateral Fibula (this topic)**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Distal Posterolateral Fibula**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**Distal Posterior Fibula**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Indications",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The Pangea Fibula Plating System is indicated for the internal fixation and stabilization of fibula bone fractures and osteotomies in normal and osteopenic bone, including",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "diaphyseal, metaphyseal, epiphyseal, extra- and intra-articular fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "non-unions, malunions, and deformities",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "periprosthetic fractures",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Design",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "**Variable Angle Locking Technology**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Pangea’s variable angle locking technology uses a CoCr locking screw, which is harder than the Ti6Al4V ELI plate, allowing for the screwhead’s threads to form a definitive plate-screw interface in the plate's locking hole by engaging the softer, Ti6Al4V material.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423205/images/4432eecf-e3a3-4258-bc7b-e4ee04950594_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_11.32.37_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "this technology allows the user to aim and lock the screw into the plate within a true 30° cone of the predetermined hole trajectory. The variable angle drill guide provided with the system offers guidance with respect to the limit of the 30° cone. The locking mechanism remains functional for up to three attempts at locking screw insertion.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "circular holes that accommodate screws are universal, accepting non-locking screws and locking screws within a 30° cone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**1.6mm K-Wire Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**3 Dedicated Syndesmotic Holes**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "offset for fixation and recessed to accommodate buttons.",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423205/images/b5f9c420-51de-4629-8623-d0293ff1595e_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_11.33.44_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Anterior Distal Hole**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "designed to allow for capture of syndesmotic avulsion fracture fragment",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Distal 2.7mm Cluster**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "designed to allow for increased fixation options",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/423205/images/9d21f3dc-3168-47fc-b6e0-13ee67ab21a7_screenshot_2025-01-08_at_11.32.45_am.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**2 Distal K-Wire Holes**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "designed for distal placement",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "-1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approach",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Surgeons may use a standard lateral or posterolateral approach to the distal fibula.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Plate Placement",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "-1",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "The distal portion of the plate should be placed so the 2.7mm cluster is centered over the lateral aspect of the metaphyseal fibula (Confirm there is no overhang over the edge of the distal fibula)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "When positioned correctly, the distal cluster should cup the lateral metaphyseal fibula",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The proximal aspect of the plate should be centered on the lateral face of the shaft of the fibula",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "-1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
